================================================================================
  ENGINEERING REPORT: 8-BIT ADDER ARCHITECTURE ANALYSIS & COMPARISON
================================================================================
Project:      Design and Verification of an 8-bit ALU with Register File in Verilog,
              and Adder Architecture Analysis
Author:       Senior RTL Design & FPGA Engineer
Date:         2026-11-14
Architectures Analyzed:
  1. Ripple Carry Adder (RCA)
  2. Carry Look-Ahead Adder (CLA)
  3. Carry Select Adder (CSLA)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. ARCHITECTURAL OVERVIEW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A. Ripple Carry Adder (RCA):
   - Principle: Cascades 8 1-bit full adders in series.
   - Carry Propagation: The carry out of stage i serves as the carry in to stage i+1.
   - Logic Equations:
     * Sum_i  = A_i ^ B_i ^ Cin_i
     * Cout_i = (A_i & B_i) | (Cin_i & (A_i ^ B_i))
   - Critical Path: Traverses from Cin through all 8 full adder carry-out circuits to Cout_7.

B. Carry Look-Ahead Adder (CLA):
   - Principle: Pre-computes carries using bit Generate (G_i = A_i & B_i) and
     bit Propagate (P_i = A_i ^ B_i) signals.
   - Hierarchical Partitioning: Two 4-bit CLA blocks with block-level lookahead:
     * Block 0 computes bits [3:0], generating Block Generate (G_blk0) and Block Propagate (P_blk0).
     * Intermediate carry C4 = G_blk0 | (P_blk0 & Cin) is calculated in constant 2-level gate delay.
     * Block 1 computes bits [7:4] using C4 as carry-in.
     * Final carry Cout = G_blk1 | (P_blk1 & C4).

C. Carry Select Adder (CSLA):
   - Principle: Conditional speculative computation.
   - Partitioning:
     * Lower 4 bits [3:0] are computed normally, generating Sum[3:0] and C4.
     * Upper 4 bits [7:4] are computed in parallel using TWO duplicate adders:
       - Speculative Adder 0 computes Sum_c0 and Cout_c0 assuming Cin = 0.
       - Speculative Adder 1 computes Sum_c1 and Cout_c1 assuming Cin = 1.
     * Multiplexers (2:1) select the upper sum and carry once C4 arrives from the lower stage.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THEORETICAL COMPLEXITY & GATE-LEVEL METRICS (ASIC / DISCRETE GATES)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Parameter                | Ripple Carry (RCA) | Carry Lookahead (CLA) | Carry Select (CSLA)
-------------------------+--------------------+-----------------------+--------------------
Full Adder / Core Cells  | 8 Full Adders      | 2x 4-bit CLA blocks   | 3x 4-bit Adders + MUX
Total 2-Input Gate Count | ~40 gates          | ~64 gates             | ~78 gates
Asymptotic Area          | O(N)               | O(N) or O(N log N)    | O(1.5 * N)
Asymptotic Delay         | O(N)               | O(log N)              | O(sqrt(N))
8-Bit Logical Depth      | 16 gate levels     | 4 to 6 gate levels    | 6 gate levels
Relative Silicon Area    | 1.0x (Baseline)    | 1.6x                  | 1.95x
Relative Delay (ASIC)    | 1.0x (Slowest)     | 0.35x (Fastest)       | 0.45x (Intermediate)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. FPGA IMPLEMENTATION CHARACTERISTICS (XILINX 7-SERIES / ARTIX-7)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

In modern FPGA architectures such as Xilinx Artix-7 (28nm SLICE structure):

1. The Role of Dedicated Carry Chains (CARRY4 / CARRY8):
   - Modern FPGAs feature hardened dedicated carry-chain hardware (`CARRY4` primitives).
   - The carry multiplexer inside a SLICE has an ultra-fast sub-nanosecond propagation
     delay (~15 to 25 ps per bit), routing carries via dedicated hardwired vertical lines
     without entering the general-purpose FPGA routing interconnect.
   - For word lengths <= 32 bits, a synthesized HDL adder using behavioral `+` or an RCA
     structure mapped directly to `CARRY4` often yields lower delay and lower LUT utilization
     than a soft-logic CLA.

2. Soft Fabric Mapping of CLA and CSLA:
   - When instantiated structurally (as done in this project to demonstrate classical digital
     design principles), the CLA equations map into 6-input LUTs (LUT6).
   - In CSLA, duplicate upper adders require additional LUTs and 2:1 multiplexers (using LUT6
     or MUXF7 primitives), increasing overall LUT consumption.

3. Estimated FPGA Synthesis Metrics for 8-bit Adders (xc7a35tcpg236-1):
   Architecture               | Estimated LUTs | Estimated Logic Levels | Estimated Path Delay
   ---------------------------+----------------+------------------------+---------------------
   Ripple Carry Adder (RCA)   | 8 LUTs         | 4 to 5                 | ~2.4 ns
   Carry Lookahead Adder(CLA) | 12 - 14 LUTs   | 2 to 3                 | ~1.6 ns
   Carry Select Adder (CSLA)  | 16 - 18 LUTs   | 3                      | ~1.9 ns

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. ENGINEERING TRADE-OFF CONCLUSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- ASIC Implementation: CLA delivers the superior speed-area trade-off for medium bit-widths
  (8-bit to 32-bit). CSLA provides excellent latency reduction in wide adders (32-bit to 64-bit)
  by trading off ~50-80% more silicon area.
- FPGA Implementation: For narrow widths (8-bit), the hardened CARRY4 primitives in Vivado
  outperform soft fabric implementations. However, structural CLA provides the most deterministic
  and minimal combinational logic level count in pure LUT logic fabric.
